<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    CROSS-LEVEL UTILITY RISERS - shared base.

    A riser is placed on one level and pairs with its twin on the level above or below,
    joining the two levels' networks into one. The pairing mechanic is not built yet
    (§30b-d); until it is, a riser behaves as an ordinary member of its own level's
    network, which is a coherent thing to build and cannot break a save.

    ⚠ THIS BASE LIVES IN Defs/ AND IS ALWAYS LOADED, while most of its children live under
    Compat/ and load only with their host mod (see LoadFolders.xml). That is fine and is
    the point: an abstract parent naming only vanilla types is safe everywhere, so every
    gated child can inherit shape, cost and stats from one place instead of repeating them.

    ⚠ A RISER GOES *IN* A WALL, NOT BESIDE ONE. `canPlaceOverWall` + PlaceWorker_ABOnWall,
    modelled directly on VTE_WallMountedVent and vanilla's Vent. The building occupies the
    WALL'S OWN cell and faces out of it into the room.

    ⚠ THE EARLIER `isAttachment` VERSION WAS WRONG AND COULD NOT PLACE AT ALL. RimWorld has
    two unrelated wall-mounted mechanisms:
      isAttachment  - occupies the OPEN cell beside a wall; Rotation points INTO the wall;
                      the wall must declare `supportsWallAttachments`, which ORDINARY COLONY
                      WALLS DO NOT. Only a handful of natural and Anomaly structures do, so
                      attachments silently refuse to place on a normal wall.
      canPlaceOverWall - occupies the WALL's cell; Rotation points OUT into the room.
    Vanilla Vent, VTE's WallMountedVent and WallMountedCooler all use the second.

    ⚠ SO ROTATION IS ORDINARY FACING, AND `_south` IS THE FRONT VIEW AGAIN. Because the
    unit faces out of the wall rather than into it, the usual RimWorld convention applies:
    _south faces the player and shows the front. An `isAttachment` riser would have needed
    the inverted convention; this one does not.

    ⚠ NON-EDIFICE ON PURPOSE. `isEdifice=false` is what lets the riser share its cell with
    the wall instead of replacing it, and `clearBuildingArea=false` is what stops placement
    demolishing that wall. Both are load-bearing - drop either and the riser either cannot
    be placed or eats the structure it is supposed to sit inside.
  -->
  <ThingDef Name="AB_RiserBase" ParentName="BuildingBase" Abstract="True">
    <thingClass>Building</thingClass>
    <category>Building</category>
    <size>(1,1)</size>
    <!--
      ⚠ `Item`, NOT `BuildingOnTop`, AND THAT IS TUNED RATHER THAN CHOSEN. A riser shares a
      cell with the wall it is mounted in, and at BuildingOnTop the wall still won the draw
      order on some facings, hiding the unit. Item sits one step higher and clears it.
      Known cost: floor items share this layer, so an item dropped in the same cell can
      z-fight with the riser. Acceptable - the cell is a wall, so items rarely land there.
    -->
    <altitudeLayer>Item</altitudeLayer>
    <passability>PassThroughOnly</passability>
    <fillPercent>0.75</fillPercent>
    <blockWind>true</blockWind>
    <blockLight>false</blockLight>
    <canOverlapZones>false</canOverlapZones>
    <rotatable>true</rotatable>
    <selectable>true</selectable>
    <clearBuildingArea>false</clearBuildingArea>
    <terrainAffordanceNeeded>Light</terrainAffordanceNeeded>
    <placeWorkers>
      <li>AsAboveSoBelow.PlaceWorker_ABOnWall</li>
    </placeWorkers>
    <drawPlaceWorkersWhileSelected>true</drawPlaceWorkersWhileSelected>
    <constructEffect>ConstructMetal</constructEffect>
    <repairEffect>Repair</repairEffect>
    <leaveResourcesWhenKilled>true</leaveResourcesWhenKilled>
    <designationHotKey>Misc4</designationHotKey>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>120</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>1100</WorkToBuild>
      <Flammability>0.4</Flammability>
      <Beauty>-3</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costList>
      <Steel>35</Steel>
      <ComponentIndustrial>1</ComponentIndustrial>
    </costList>
    <building>
      <isEdifice>false</isEdifice>
      <isInert>true</isInert>
      <canPlaceOverWall>true</canPlaceOverWall>
      <ai_chillDestination>false</ai_chillDestination>
    </building>
    <!--
      ⚠ THREE FILES, NOT FOUR. Graphic_Multi.Init falls back to _east with westFlipped when
      _west is absent, so north/east/south is a complete set - the same thing Dubs Bad
      Hygiene ships for its own boilers and vents. A hand-authored _west would only be an
      opportunity for the two side views to drift apart.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠ THE SPRITE IS DRAWN ALMOST A WHOLE CELL TOWARD THE FACING DIRECTION, AND WITHOUT
      THAT IT COVERS THE WALL IT IS MOUNTED IN. The riser occupies the WALL's cell, so a
      centred sprite paints straight over the wall and the wall visually disappears -
      leaving a box floating in a gap. Offsetting toward the open side draws it on the
      wall's face instead: the wall stays visible and the unit reads as attached to it.

      0.9 is not a guess - it is what vanilla's WallLamp/TorchWallLamp and VTE's
      ACUnitWall, WallMountedHeater, WallMountedVent and WallMountedCooler all use. Both
      wall-mounting mechanisms converge on the same number because both are solving this
      same overlap.

      ⚠ SOUTH IS ZERO WHILE THE OTHER THREE ARE 0.9, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
      Measured in game with the texture tuner, not derived. RimWorld's camera is top-down
      with a slight forward tilt, so the two directions are not symmetric on screen: a unit
      on the wall's SOUTH face is already on the side facing the camera and reads as mounted
      with no offset at all, while north, east and west are on faces turned away and have to
      be pushed clear of the wall sprite to be seen. Restoring south to -0.9 "for symmetry"
      pushes it a full cell into the room.

      Sign convention for the other three is "toward the rotation", and rotation points OUT
      of the wall (see the note above), so north is +z, east +x, west -x.

      Tunable in ONE place: every riser inherits this because RimWorld merges graphicData
      field-by-field down the ParentName chain, so a child redefining texPath and
      graphicClass keeps these offsets.
    -->
    <graphicData>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Multi</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>(0.8,0.8)</drawSize>
      <drawOffsetNorth>(0,0,0.9)</drawOffsetNorth>
      <drawOffsetSouth>(0,0,0)</drawOffsetSouth>
      <drawOffsetEast>(0.9,0,0)</drawOffsetEast>
      <drawOffsetWest>(-0.9,0,0)</drawOffsetWest>
      <damageData>
        <rect>(0.1,0.1,0.8,0.8)</rect>
      </damageData>
    </graphicData>
    <!-- Rimefeller and Rimatomics gate their own flick-rebuild on `is Building_Valve`, so
         our breakers need this to invalidate their nets. No-op everywhere else. -->
    <comps>
      <li Class="CompProperties">
        <compClass>AsAboveSoBelow.CompABRiserSwitch</compClass>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </ThingDef>


  <ThingDef ParentName="AB_RiserBase">
    <defName>AB_PowerJunction</defName>
    <label>power junction box</label>
    <description>A wall-mounted power junction box. It bridges its network to a matching breaker box in the same cell one level above and one level below, merging those levels' networks into this one.\n\nBuild the junction where the machinery is; build a breaker on each level you want to reach.</description>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Building/Risers/AB_PowerJunction</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Multi</graphicClass>
    </graphicData>
    <designationCategory>Power</designationCategory>
    <researchPrerequisites>
      <li>Electricity</li>
    </researchPrerequisites>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>120</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>1600</WorkToBuild>
      <Flammability>0.4</Flammability>
      <Beauty>-3</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costList>
      <Steel>60</Steel>
      <ComponentIndustrial>2</ComponentIndustrial>
    </costList>
    <comps>
      <li Class="CompProperties_Power">
        <compClass>CompPowerTransmitter</compClass>
        <transmitsPower>true</transmitsPower>
      </li>
    </comps>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="AsAboveSoBelow.ABRiserExt">
        <network>Power</network>
        <role>Junction</role>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="AB_RiserBase">
    <defName>AB_PowerBreaker</defName>
    <thingClass>AsAboveSoBelow.Building_ABPowerBreaker</thingClass>
    <label>power breaker box</label>
    <description>A wall-mounted power breaker box. Place it in the same cell as a junction box one level above or below and the two levels' networks merge.\n\nFlick it off to cut the link without deconstructing anything.</description>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Building/Risers/AB_PowerBreaker</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Multi</graphicClass>
    </graphicData>
    <designationCategory>Power</designationCategory>
    <researchPrerequisites>
      <li>Electricity</li>
    </researchPrerequisites>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>120</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>900</WorkToBuild>
      <Flammability>0.4</Flammability>
      <Beauty>-3</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costList>
      <Steel>25</Steel>
      <ComponentIndustrial>1</ComponentIndustrial>
    </costList>
    <comps>
      <li Class="CompProperties_Power">
        <compClass>CompPowerTransmitter</compClass>
        <transmitsPower>true</transmitsPower>
      </li>
      <li Class="CompProperties_Flickable" />
    </comps>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="AsAboveSoBelow.ABRiserExt">
        <network>Power</network>
        <role>Breaker</role>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
